#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175687 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "d15d6003-a4dd-599f-987d-289508968ac7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10105" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 7$.\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by*\n$f(x)=\\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}} \\\\\n&= (θ^{x_{1}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{1}}) \\cdot (θ^{x_{2}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{2}})⋯(θ^{x_{n}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{n}}) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{∑(1 - x_{i})} \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{n - ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= θ^{S}(1 - θ)^{n - S}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i=7\n$$\n\nand $n=10$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= θ^{7}(1 - θ)^{10 - 7} \\\\\n&= θ^{7}(1 - θ)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "3e3ca456-a46a-5a5b-9cf6-07caad631ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11117" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(4,\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta$. For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12}$, you're given that\n$\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 22$.\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C$.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n$f(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{n-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1,2,\\ldots, n$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 12$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}f(x; θ) = (\\frac{4}{x})θ^{x}(1 - θ)^{4 - x}, & x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(\\frac{4}{x_{i}})θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{i}} \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{4}{x_{1}})θ^{x_{1}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{1}}) \\cdot ((\\frac{4}{x_{2}})θ^{x_{2}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{2}})⋯((\\frac{4}{x_{n}})θ^{x_{n}}(1 - θ)^{4 - x_{n}}) \\\\\n&= Cθ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{∑(4 - x_{i})} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{4n - ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{S}(1 - θ)^{4n - S}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\prod\\limits_{i=1}^n {4 \\choose x_i}\n$$\n\nis a constant and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i = 22\n$$\n\nand $n = 12$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= Cθ^{22}(1 - θ)^{4(12) - 22} \\\\\n&= Cθ^{22}(1 - θ)^{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "9f08cf84-3e3b-5c86-adb6-622bc51a15f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11118" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta$. Find the likelihood function of the sample $0, 2, 3, 1, 1$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta$, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\\,\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)\\frac{θ^{x_{i}}e^{- θ}}{x_{i}!} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{θ^{x_{1}}e^{- θ}}{x_{1}!}) \\cdot (\\frac{θ^{x_{2}}e^{- θ}}{x_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{θ^{x_{n}}e^{- θ}}{x_{n}!}) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}} \\cdot e^{- ∑θ} \\cdot ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(1)/((x_{i})!) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}} \\cdot e^{-nθ} \\cdot (1)/(∏_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i})!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{C}θ^{S}e^{-nθ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\prod\\limits_{i=1}^{n} (x_i)\n$$\n\nis a constant and\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 0 + 2 + 3 + 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 7 \\\\\nC &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i})! \\\\\n&= 0! \\cdot 2! \\cdot 3! \\cdot 1! \\cdot 1! \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= \\frac{1}{12}θ^{7}e^{-5θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f69b12e5-6e94-5739-8bac-2236468f5e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12θ^{16}e^{-18θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174491} {:answer/id #uuid "174af438-cbee-5017-8bea-81d95694dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}θ^{2}e^{-16θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174492} {:answer/id #uuid "18fd9e7e-4094-5f72-9f22-586b1c442ff9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6θ^{18}e^{-2θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174493} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1bb1e5-f845-525c-8b2e-0d5f30f623ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6θ^{2}e^{-18θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174494} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9cec40-251a-5532-98a2-b4bb5208bb69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}θ^{18}e^{-16θ}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174495}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d1803602-4fb6-5082-a851-eb54e4e8a4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174490}] :question/id #uuid "ab6f3ebb-34cb-52f6-a546-298076d55271" :question/math-academy-id "q-143472" :question/problem "Suppose $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_{i}∼Po(θ)$ and unknown rate parameter $θ$. For a particular sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, …, x_{16}$, you're given that\n$∑_(i = 1)^(16)x_{i} = 18, ∏_(i = 1)^(16)(x_{i})! = 6$.\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by*\n$f(x) = \\frac{θ^{x}e^{- θ}}{x!}, x = 0, 1, 2, …$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
